this is jezreel crater  today it is a hostile landscape too  bitterly cold and dry  for any living thing to survive a few  billion years ago however  jezreel might have looked similar to  earth's own lake salda  in turkey back then mars owned a thicker  atmosphere  it warmed the planet enough for water to  flow and lakes to form in craters like  jezreel  such conditions could have supported the  beginnings of life  but did this warm environment last long  enough to foster microbes  and why did mars change so drastically  nasa's latest mars rover perseverance  will investigate these questions  by collecting martian rock and soil  samples that may one day  return to earth on the outside  perseverance might look familiar the  rover's design  borrows much from its older sibling  curiosity  but on the inside perseverance holds  something new  the sample caching system this  sophisticated machine  will drill into the martian surface and  remove  image and store chalk sized cores of  rock and soil  eventually the rover will cash more than  30 cores  for a future mission to pick up and send  to earth for intensive study  we can't learn much from these cores  until they make their journey home  but perseverance has more than one trick  up its sleeve  the rover is outfitted with tools to  discern the chemical makeup of rocks and  soil  by mapping the distribution of organic  molecules in a rock  these tools could help scientists  differentiate between organic debris  from meteorite impacts  and the remains of microbial life  perseverance will spend its first two  years exploring jezreel and collecting  samples on a meandering path from lake  bed to shore  the rover's primary mission is to seek  signs of past life  but researchers will also use the data  to investigate the geologic and climatic  history of mars  beginning its journey on the dark crater  floor perseverance will likely find  a volcanic landscape frozen in time  if the surface is in fact fossilized  lava flow  researchers may discern the lake's age  thanks to radiometric  dating next the rover should encounter  fine-grained clay at the edge of the  delta which sits at the mouth of an  ancient river that fed into the lake  on earth these clays often preserve  biosignatures  so here perseverance will look for  organic compounds that could betray the  existence  of past microorganisms continuing its  quest for ancient life  the rover will traverse rough sandstone  the grains of this sandstone might be  billions of years old having flowed down  river from some of the oldest terrain on  mars  if this is the case researchers could  study the magnetic signatures of these  early rocks to unveil the history of the  martian magnetic field  one theory suggests that the field  shielded a thick atmospheric blanket  that once warmed the red planet  and when the field failed the atmosphere  dissipated and the climate changed  to culminate its mission the rover will  seek the carbonate-rich shores of the  ancient lake  these limestone-like carbonates are  known on earth to preserve stromatolites  mounds formed by bacterial growth thus  this is an ideal place to look for such  geologic time capsules of past life  scientists are also interested in these  minerals because they're scarce  on the surface of mars despite an  atmosphere that today  is 95 carbon dioxide studying these  rocks  could reshape our limited understanding  of the martian carbon cycle perseverance  is the first mars rover designed to seek  signs of ancient life  but that's not the missions only first  one instrument  will record sound on the martian surface  for the very first time  another the ingenuity helicopter will  attempt to demonstrate the first powered  flight on another planet  a challenging feat since the martian  atmosphere is 100 times thinner  than earth's if successful ingenuity  could pave the way for future flight  driven exploration  on the red planet more than 50 years ago  humans retrieved rocks and soil from the  moon for the very first  time these samples completely reshaped  our understanding of the moon's  formation  and continue to be studied today we can  only begin to imagine what similar  samples from mars  might teach us about the red planet our  solar system  and perhaps life itself  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

